{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01X7tXN95Z8BqYS4qbqvspse","type":"tool_result","content":"# Plan for #312\n\n## Implementation\n\nDocs only: edit `docs/proposal.md`, no Go code, no new file (the owner said the proposal plus the decision comment on this issue are the record). The worker must match on text, not line numbers, since they have drifted. Source of the content: the design comment (decisions 1-12), the gaps comment, and the owner's answers on this issue. Where the owner's answers differ from the earlier plan comment, the owner's answers win.\n\n### Steps\n\n1. **Lock mentions.** In \"Claims need a single owner\", replace \"a Kubernetes Lease in-cluster\" with: `flock` in both modes; in-cluster the lock file is on the `-home` PVC, which is `ReadWriteOncePod`, with `replicas: 1` and the `Recreate` strategy (decision 9). In \"Where it runs\", replace \"a Lease lock in place of the `flock`\" with \"the same `flock` on a `ReadWriteOncePod` volume\".\n2. **Retries paragraph.** Keep \"node eviction under Kubernetes\" and name its cause `evicted` (transient; gap 4: new `Status.Transient` and `closeout.CauseEvicted` come in later parts, say so).\n3. **Executors table, Kubernetes column** (keep 3 columns per row; the Labels and Good for rows stay):\n   - Workspace: the same prepared worktree as local, packed into a tarball that the pod fetches from the job API into an `emptyDir` with `sizeLimit`; the result is pushed back as a full tree.\n   - Runtime: a bare Pod (no Job object): init container `fetch`, agent container under `ard job-exec`, and a plain second container `upload` that waits for the done marker, uploads and exits (not a native sidecar, so no Kubernetes 1.29+ minimum); default `ard-agent` image; hard timeout.\n   - Heartbeat: the controller follows `pods/log` into the host log; log mtime.\n   - Logs: followed into `~/.ard/logs/...`; stdout from the tee file shipped in the result.\n4. **New paragraph \"Kubernetes-executor details\"** right after the table, same style as \"Local-executor details\". Cover: the flow; decisions 1-8 and 10-12 (volume, push result, pod shape and key isolation, key lifecycle, exit code from the kubelet and `Gone` never `Exited` 0, untar rules, listener, logs and heartbeat, cluster, REST client without client-go, images); the pod paths `/job`, `/work`, `/role`, `/sidecar`, `/ard-config`, `/ard-out`, `/ard-bin`; the result layout (input roots `work/ role/ config/ sidecar/`, result roots `work/ out/`, stdout at `out/stdout` outside `work/`; gap 2); the deadline arithmetic (gap 13: `h.Deadline = Start + Timeout`, job-exec deadline `h.Deadline - min(2m, Timeout/10)`, pod `activeDeadlineSeconds = Timeout + UploadGrace` (2 min), key revoked at `Deadline + UploadGrace`, job-exec exits 124 which maps to `TimedOut`); the exit code stored in `<id>.done` at accept (gap 14); upload grace for the `upload` container; pod name limit (`ard-<id>` at most 63, ids over 59 rejected, lowercased ids; gap 12); `job-exec` reaps orphans as PID 1 (gap 18); handles of the other executor kind report `Gone` (gap 15); orphan-pod sweep scoped by an `ard/repo` hash label (gap 16); default input size limits agreeing with the `emptyDir` `sizeLimit` (gap 19, name the defaults as \"from the tarball limits plus the job resources flags\"); the executor is not an effect. State the owner's answers: TLS option A (self-signed ECDSA P-256 cert generated in memory at `ard serve` start, SAN the Service DNS name, SHA-256 of the leaf pinned in each per-job Secret as `fingerprint`, verified with `VerifyConnection`; restart rotates it); `--job-api-tls=self-signed|off`, `off` only under dry-run (mark this flag as planned for part 4); cluster settings (jobs namespace, API address, images) are `ard serve` flags set by the chart, not `.ard/config.yaml` (gap 6: the executor is fixed at startup, a later config with a different `executor` is a config error until restart); the agent image is public under `ghcr.io/albertcbrown/ard` and must hold no secrets (an image test checks this); #317 is split into the API layer and the executor.\n5. **Job TTL sentence.** Replace \"the Kubernetes `Job` gets a short TTL\" with: a Kubernetes job's pod and per-job Secret are deleted (no Job object, no TTL).\n6. **Configuration section.** (a) Dockerfile bullet: under `executor: k8s` every role runs the default `ard-agent` image and a role `Dockerfile` is ignored until in-cluster builds exist (own issue); name the affected starter roles, i.e. the scanners `cve`, `docs`, `error-codes`, `lint`, `missing-tests`, `trufflehog` (`internal/roles/scanners/*/Dockerfile`) and any worker whose tests need a toolchain, and say the rollout must either put their tools in the public image or keep them off under k8s (gap 7). (b) Config yaml: `executor: local # local | k8s; fixed at startup`; add one sentence after the block saying cluster settings are `ard serve` flags set by the chart.\n7. **Guardrails.** Replace the egress clause \"in Kubernetes a network policy limits egress to the model API and package registries\" with: in Kubernetes job egress is DNS, the job API and the internet minus private ranges and 169.254.169.254 (matching the #276 posture). Add four bullets after that bullet: **Job API key** (decision 4), **Job API listener** (decision 7 plus TLS option A), **Result untar is the boundary** (decision 6), **Cluster** (decision 10). The Cluster bullet lists the residual risks: the model credential sits in a per-job Secret in etcd, so it relies on etcd encryption at rest (gap 17); the tee'd stdout is agent-writable, so the agent can forge its own usage figures, the same exposure local Docker has today, accepted by the owner, exit code still from the kubelet, revisit when split log streams are available (gap 1); pod egress reaches the whole internet; Pod Security `restricted` relies on the cluster's admission. Drop the \"key added to the redaction set by value\" requirement from the text of decision 4 or mark it not planned (gap 5), and say so in the Open questions entry.\n8. **Open questions.** Append `- [x] **Kubernetes executor (#54).**` with a one-paragraph decided summary linking #54, the design comment and #312, plus the owner's answers. Add one unchecked `- [ ]` entry for each gap the owner has not settled: gap 5 (redaction of the key), gap 3 now moot under the plain upload container (record the grace period instead), gap 11 (worktree registration after the swap: prune versus export tree), gap 15/16 only if the details paragraph does not already decide them. Gaps 1, 4, 6, 13 and the owner's five answers are recorded as decided.\n9. No change to `docs/layout.md`. The Delivery \"Agent image\" bullet says the image \"is never published\". Leave that sentence for #320 (the owner assigned the change there), but add to the details paragraph that the k8s agent image will be public, so the two are not read as a contradiction; the worker notes the pending conflict in its PR.\n\n### Test plan\n\nNo Go code; run `make lint test` (must stay green). Checks:\n- `grep -n \"Lease\\|short TTL\\|Kubernetes \\`Job\\`\" docs/proposal.md` finds nothing.\n- `grep -n \"native sidecar\" docs/proposal.md` appears only to say it is not used.\n- Every row of the Executors table still has 3 cells.\n- Links resolve only to issues #54 and #312 and the design comment URL.\n- No remaining statement that egress is limited to the model API and package registries.\n\n### Risk\n\nLow. Docs only; no protected path (`docs/proposal.md` is not in `merge.protected_paths`), no interfaces, no migration. The only risk is drift: later parts are planned against these answers (TLS A, plain upload container, stdout from the tee file, executor fixed at startup, `evicted` cause).\n\n### Size\n\nS to M: about 100-140 changed lines, but the paragraphs are long single lines, so the diff may count more. File touched: `docs/proposal.md`.\n---\nrole: lead\nstatus: done\nattempt: 1\nnext: [triage:engineer]\nworker: engineer\n---\nThis is a docs-only change: it records the approved Kubernetes executor design as a decision and updates the matching sections of docs/proposal.md. No code changes. The scope is clear and the owner has answered the open questions in the comments, so the engineer plans the edits and the engineer worker makes them.\n\n## Routing\n- triage:engineer: turn the comment plan and the owner's decisions into exact edit steps for docs/proposal.md, and match them to the file's current line numbers.\n- worker engineer: edit the documentation (decision record, Executors table, cleanup text, lock mentions, Guardrails).\n\n## Open issues\n- The design comment on the parent issue holds decisions 1 to 12, and a gaps file is also referenced. Neither is in the repository, and the worker cannot fetch them. If the engineer cannot rebuild every decision from the issue comments, a maintainer should paste the decision text into the issue or the repository.\n- The comment plan describes the upload step as a native sidecar, but the owner's later decision chose a plain second container. The plan should follow the owner's decision.\n- The line numbers cited for docs/proposal.md have drifted. For example, the Job TTL text is now near line 317 and the second Lease mention near line 544.\n- No architect, product-manager or documentation-planner triage roles exist, so only the engineer plans this. The owner already approved the design.\n5:## Summary\n22:## Architecture\n34:        E[\"Executors: max 3 pods, local or k8s\"]\n44:## Stack\n48:## Label state machine\n89:**Claims need a single owner, not atomic labels.** GitHub labels are not a lock: two pollers can both add `:running`. Phase 1 runs one controller per repo behind a host lock (`flock` locally, a Kubernetes Lease in-cluster), so the controller is the only claimer. If you ever run several, the claimer re-reads the issue's label timeline and backs off unless its own `labeled` event is first.\n93:**Retries are explicit.** Every retry, automatic or human, is a new job with its own id (and attempt 1). A human retries a failed job by removing `ard:executor:failed`. The controller retries on its own once, only for a transient cause it can name: a rate limit, a clone failure, a controller restart (the job was orphaned, gone or never started across it), or node eviction under Kubernetes. The failed job then gets no `ard:executor:failed` and keeps its request label, so the next claim runs it again; that claim is linked to the failed job (`retry_of` in local state), so cost and causes can be traced. An automatic retry that fails again is marked failed. A usage-limit stop is not a failure: it closes out as `usage-limit`, with no `ard:executor:failed` and the request label kept, and it neither uses up nor grants the automatic retry. The re-run after the reset is a new job linked to the stopped one through `after_limit` in local state, which also carries `retry_of` over, so a limit stop of an automatic retry does not earn a second retry. Its comment says the issue is paused for the usage limit until a time. A throttle that the CLI says is \"not your usage limit\" is an ordinary rate-limit retry, not a usage-limit stop. Thresholds and early warnings are [#63](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/issues/63).\n95:## The .ard/ contract\n152:## Open issues\n166:## Scanners\n234:**The trufflehog-history scanner** (`scanners/trufflehog-history`) runs `trufflehog git file://$ARD_HISTORY --bare` with the same flags plus no `--since-commit`, `--max-depth` or `--branch`, over every fetched ref, with the same hash salt, so a value has the same fingerprint as at HEAD. A scanner role asks for history by holding an empty file named `history` in its directory (`Role.ReadsHistory`; there is no config key, so an override cannot grant it). The executor then sets `Job.History` (scanner stage only) and mounts the controller's bare clone read-only at `/history` in Docker (host mode uses the clone path) and sets `ARD_HISTORY`. The mount includes unmerged PR branches. Commit, author and timestamp are dropped; only the detector, file, line and hash leave the scan. It never autocloses, because history does not go away. A Kubernetes executor must provide a read-only copy at the same place.\n240:## Triage loop\n281:## Executors and the Reaper\n285:Local-executor details: the role directory (and its `Dockerfile`) is mounted from the config snapshot at the job's SHA, not from the working tree. The job worktree is always detached at a SHA resolved from the job's ref (the executor never checks out a branch; the committer owns branch worktrees). The controller's clone is a bare repository ard owns at `~/.ard/repos/<owner>/<repo>.git` (lowercased), with no remote, no stored credential and auto-gc off (`gc.auto=0`, `maintenance.auto=false`). `ard serve` creates it on first start and seeds it with one fetch of `main` (up to 30 min; if that fails, serve starts paused and the next start retries), and later starts reuse it. Config loads and committer lanes fetch into it, and every committer and executor worktree is cut from it (lanes under `~/.ard/work/<owner>/<repo>/lanes/`). `ard serve` never writes to the operator's checkout: it reads it only for the `origin` fallback of `--repo` and the local `paused` key. To rebuild a damaged clone, stop serve, `rm -rf` it and restart. Upgrading from a version that used the checkout: ard deletes its old lane and job worktree directories without running git there, so the old `refs/ard/*` refs and the dangling worktree registrations stay in the checkout until you remove them (`git for-each-ref --format='delete %(refname)' refs/ard/ | git update-ref --stdin; git worktree prune`). Every `git worktree add`, `remove` and `prune` on the controller's clone, by the executor and the committer's lanes alike, takes one in-process lock keyed by the clone's common git directory, because git's worktree bookkeeping is not safe under concurrent admin commands; fetches, commits and pushes do not take it. Each such command also has a 30 s deadline, and cleanup runs even when the job's context is cancelled, so one hung git cannot stall every lane. Do not run `git worktree prune` (or add/remove) in `~/.ard/repos/<owner>/<repo>.git` by hand while `ard serve` runs: the lock is per process and cannot cover a human's git. The operator's own checkout is free to use. The host keeps two files per job: `<base>.log` (stdout and stderr combined, the heartbeat source and the committed log) and `<base>.stdout` (stdout only, parsed by the adapter). A read-only job (`ReadOnly`, used by scanners) gets the worktree mounted `:ro` under Docker, or `chmod -R a-w` on the host. The heartbeat is the log file's mtime while the PID is alive; liveness alone is not a beat. Phase 0 local jobs do not survive a controller restart: the pipes belong to the controller, so after a restart the job reports `orphaned` (PID alive, start time verified) or `gone`, and the Reaper kills and fails it. The executor is not an effect: it never calls GitHub or commits, so jobs run normally under `--dry-run`. Sidecar auth: the sidecar fetch runs in the controller with the GitHub App installation token in a host-scoped `http.<url>.extraheader` passed through git's environment, never argv; the job receives plain files and no credential, and the cache stores no remote and no token. The App must be installed on the sidecar repo with contents:read. Host-mode jobs can reach `~/.ard/sidecars`, but it holds no credential. Host-mode jobs run as the controller's uid: they can read the GitHub App key and `~/.config/gh` by absolute path, write the controller's clone, and undo `chmod a-w`. Host mode is allowed for every role ([#84](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/issues/84)), but a role that reads untrusted input (every triage, worker and reviewer role, and an LLM scanner) warns loudly when it has no `Dockerfile`; deterministic scanners are exempt, and nothing warns under `executor: k8s`, where every job runs in a container. `ard config check` and the first successful config load of `ard serve` (and any role a later config adds) log one `executor.host_untrusted` WARN per such role and also write it to stderr, so it survives `--log-level`. The local executor logs the WARN again for every host-mode job of such a role and appends a `host_untrusted` row to the `events` table. Each warning lists what the job can reach, including `~/.ard` (state, config snapshots, the pause file, other jobs' worktrees and logs). The owner accepts this risk while ard runs only on owner-controlled repos, and revisits it when moving to the spark cluster, where every job runs in a container once the k8s executor (#54) lands. Until then the Gate 1 dry-run soak on spark (see Rollout and Delivery) runs `ard serve` with the local executor in a pod, and the deterministic cve scanner runs there as a host subprocess of the controller container: its role directory has no `Dockerfile`, and the `ard` image carries its pinned tools. Only deterministic scanners run in that pod: it starts `ard serve --loops scanners,stale,reaper` ([#187](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/issues/187)), so the triage, worker and reviewer loops never run there, even though `.ard/config.yaml` on `main` has agent roles. `--loops` takes a comma-separated list of loop names (scanners, stale, triage, workers, reviewers, reviewfix, merger, janitor, reaper, budget; `budget` is the status-issue notice loop, the claim gate is always on), defaults to every loop, and rejects an unknown name with exit 2. `ard serve` and `ard config check` refuse to run on git older than 2.47.0, because the committer's fetches fail on older versions.\n291:|  | Local executor | Kubernetes executor |\n317:Successful jobs clean up at close-out: the worktree is removed, the Kubernetes `Job` gets a short TTL, and logs stay 7 days.\n321:## Worker loop\n341:## Reviewer loop\n374:## Merger loop\n399:## PR watch loop\n413:## Configuration\n430:- A `Dockerfile` builds the role's image; without one the local executor runs a host subprocess and the Kubernetes executor uses the default `ard-agent` image (agent CLIs, git, common linters). A role Dockerfile should start `FROM` the local `ard-agent` image by digest or ID. A role that reads untrusted input without one triggers the host-mode warning (see Executors and the Reaper).\n435:executor: local              # local | k8s\n478:## Guardrails\n484:- **Agents hold no GitHub token.** The executor does. The agent's environment has only its model credential: an API key or a subscription OAuth token; in Kubernetes a network policy limits egress to the model API and package registries.\n500:## Gaps in the original sketch\n520:## Delivery\n532:## Rollout\n540:    P2 -->|\"Gate 3: 50% merged with no human code edits\"| P3[\"Phase 3: Scale<br/>k8s executor<br/>second repo<br/>shared roles repo<br/>budgets and digest\"]\n547:**Where it runs (decided, #5; amended for the soak, [#144](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/issues/144)).** `ard serve` runs live on the owner's Mac with the local executor until Phase 3, when it moves to the \"spark\" Kubernetes cluster with the k8s executor and a Lease lock in place of the `flock`. The Gate 1 soak week does not run on the Mac: it runs on spark as `ard serve --dry-run` (one pod, `Recreate`, state on a persistent volume), and the week counts from the first healthy pod; no other `ard serve` for the same repo runs meanwhile, so one ledger is counted. See Delivery. ard supports macOS and Linux only; Windows is unsupported.\n559:## Open questions","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"311bb2c6-9a43-45e9-b51b-8ab97c5a2cc9","uuid":"79280cb2-21e3-40ba-aefd-9c6d78e4e707","timestamp":"2026-11-24T17:41:33.380Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"# Plan for #312\n\n## Implementation\n\nDocs only: edit `docs/proposal.md`, no Go code, no new file (the owner said the proposal plus the decision comment on this issue are the record). The worker must match on text, not line numbers, since they have drifted. Source of the content: the design comment (decisions 1-12), the gaps comment, and the owner's answers on this issue. Where the owner's answers differ from the earlier plan comment, the owner's answers win.\n\n### Steps\n\n1. **Lock mentions.** In \"Claims need a single owner\", replace \"a Kubernetes Lease in-cluster\" with: `flock` in both modes; in-cluster the lock file is on the `-home` PVC, which is `ReadWriteOncePod`, with `replicas: 1` and the `Recreate` strategy (decision 9). In \"Where it runs\", replace \"a Lease lock in place of the `flock`\" with \"the same `flock` on a `ReadWriteOncePod` volume\".\n2. **Retries paragraph.** Keep \"node eviction under Kubernetes\" and name its cause `evicted` (transient; gap 4: new `Status.Transient` and `closeout.CauseEvicted` come in later parts, say so).\n3. **Executors table, Kubernetes column** (keep 3 columns per row; the Labels and Good for rows stay):\n   - Workspace: the same prepared worktree as local, packed into a tarball that the pod fetches from the job API into an `emptyDir` with `sizeLimit`; the result is pushed back as a full tree.\n   - Runtime: a bare Pod (no Job object): init container `fetch`, agent container under `ard job-exec`, and a plain second container `upload` that waits for the done marker, uploads and exits (not a native sidecar, so no Kubernetes 1.29+ minimum); default `ard-agent` image; hard timeout.\n   - Heartbeat: the controller follows `pods/log` into the host log; log mtime.\n   - Logs: followed into `~/.ard/logs/...`; stdout from the tee file shipped in the result.\n4. **New paragraph \"Kubernetes-executor details\"** right after the table, same style as \"Local-executor details\". Cover: the flow; decisions 1-8 and 10-12 (volume, push result, pod shape and key isolation, key lifecycle, exit code from the kubelet and `Gone` never `Exited` 0, untar rules, listener, logs and heartbeat, cluster, REST client without client-go, images); the pod paths `/job`, `/work`, `/role`, `/sidecar`, `/ard-config`, `/ard-out`, `/ard-bin`; the result layout (input roots `work/ role/ config/ sidecar/`, result roots `work/ out/`, stdout at `out/stdout` outside `work/`; gap 2); the deadline arithmetic (gap 13: `h.Deadline = Start + Timeout`, job-exec deadline `h.Deadline - min(2m, Timeout/10)`, pod `activeDeadlineSeconds = Timeout + UploadGrace` (2 min), key revoked at `Deadline + UploadGrace`, job-exec exits 124 which maps to `TimedOut`); the exit code stored in `<id>.done` at accept (gap 14); upload grace for the `upload` container; pod name limit (`ard-<id>` at most 63, ids over 59 rejected, lowercased ids; gap 12); `job-exec` reaps orphans as PID 1 (gap 18); handles of the other executor kind report `Gone` (gap 15); orphan-pod sweep scoped by an `ard/repo` hash label (gap 16); default input size limits agreeing with the `emptyDir` `sizeLimit` (gap 19, name the defaults as \"from the tarball limits plus the job resources flags\"); the executor is not an effect. State the owner's answers: TLS option A (self-signed ECDSA P-256 cert generated in memory at `ard serve` start, SAN the Service DNS name, SHA-256 of the leaf pinned in each per-job Secret as `fingerprint`, verified with `VerifyConnection`; restart rotates it); `--job-api-tls=self-signed|off`, `off` only under dry-run (mark this flag as planned for part 4); cluster settings (jobs namespace, API address, images) are `ard serve` flags set by the chart, not `.ard/config.yaml` (gap 6: the executor is fixed at startup, a later config with a different `executor` is a config error until restart); the agent image is public under `ghcr.io/albertcbrown/ard` and must hold no secrets (an image test checks this); #317 is split into the API layer and the executor.\n5. **Job TTL sentence.** Replace \"the Kubernetes `Job` gets a short TTL\" with: a Kubernetes job's pod and per-job Secret are deleted (no Job object, no TTL).\n6. **Configuration section.** (a) Dockerfile bullet: under `executor: k8s` every role runs the default `ard-agent` image and a role `Dockerfile` is ignored until in-cluster builds exist (own issue); name the affected starter roles, i.e. the scanners `cve`, `docs`, `error-codes`, `lint`, `missing-tests`, `trufflehog` (`internal/roles/scanners/*/Dockerfile`) and any worker whose tests need a toolchain, and say the rollout must either put their tools in the public image or keep them off under k8s (gap 7). (b) Config yaml: `executor: local # local | k8s; fixed at startup`; add one sentence after the block saying cluster settings are `ard serve` flags set by the chart.\n7. **Guardrails.** Replace the egress clause \"in Kubernetes a network policy limits egress to the model API and package registries\" with: in Kubernetes job egress is DNS, the job API and the internet minus private ranges and 169.254.169.254 (matching the #276 posture). Add four bullets after that bullet: **Job API key** (decision 4), **Job API listener** (decision 7 plus TLS option A), **Result untar is the boundary** (decision 6), **Cluster** (decision 10). The Cluster bullet lists the residual risks: the model credential sits in a per-job Secret in etcd, so it relies on etcd encryption at rest (gap 17); the tee'd stdout is agent-writable, so the agent can forge its own usage figures, the same exposure local Docker has today, accepted by the owner, exit code still from the kubelet, revisit when split log streams are available (gap 1); pod egress reaches the whole internet; Pod Security `restricted` relies on the cluster's admission. Drop the \"key added to the redaction set by value\" requirement from the text of decision 4 or mark it not planned (gap 5), and say so in the Open questions entry.\n8. **Open questions.** Append `- [x] **Kubernetes executor (#54).**` with a one-paragraph decided summary linking #54, the design comment and #312, plus the owner's answers. Add one unchecked `- [ ]` entry for each gap the owner has not settled: gap 5 (redaction of the key), gap 3 now moot under the plain upload container (record the grace period instead), gap 11 (worktree registration after the swap: prune versus export tree), gap 15/16 only if the details paragraph does not already decide them. Gaps 1, 4, 6, 13 and the owner's five answers are recorded as decided.\n9. No change to `docs/layout.md`. The Delivery \"Agent image\" bullet says the image \"is never published\". Leave that sentence for #320 (the owner assigned the change there), but add to the details paragraph that the k8s agent image will be public, so the two are not read as a contradiction; the worker notes the pending conflict in its PR.\n\n### Test plan\n\nNo Go code; run `make lint test` (must stay green). Checks:\n- `grep -n \"Lease\\|short TTL\\|Kubernetes \\`Job\\`\" docs/proposal.md` finds nothing.\n- `grep -n \"native sidecar\" docs/proposal.md` appears only to say it is not used.\n- Every row of the Executors table still has 3 cells.\n- Links resolve only to issues #54 and #312 and the design comment URL.\n- No remaining statement that egress is limited to the model API and package registries.\n\n### Risk\n\nLow. Docs only; no protected path (`docs/proposal.md` is not in `merge.protected_paths`), no interfaces, no migration. The only risk is drift: later parts are planned against these answers (TLS A, plain upload container, stdout from the tee file, executor fixed at startup, `evicted` cause).\n\n### Size\n\nS to M: about 100-140 changed lines, but the paragraphs are long single lines, so the diff may count more. File touched: `docs/proposal.md`.\n---\nrole: lead\nstatus: done\nattempt: 1\nnext: [triage:engineer]\nworker: engineer\n---\nThis is a docs-only change: it records the approved Kubernetes executor design as a decision and updates the matching sections of docs/proposal.md. No code changes. The scope is clear and the owner has answered the open questions in the comments, so the engineer plans the edits 