{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01GKpPFKZ1u5aaHpAGwzptCy","type":"tool_result","content":"**One pool of job slots, drained from the end.** At most `max_pods` jobs (default 3) run at once, shared by every loop. When a slot frees, the controller fills it in this order: reviewers, then workers, then triage, then scanners, oldest first within each. Finishing work in flight beats starting new work, so a busy day grows the scanner backlog, not the pile of half-done PRs. The merger and the Re\n\n|  | Local executor | Kubernetes executor |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Workspace | `git worktree` of the controller's clone (`~/.ard/repos/<owner>/<repo>.git`) under `~/.ard/work/<owner>/<repo>/<job-id>`; sidecar exported into the job home | `Job` with an init container that shallow-clones the branch into an `emptyDir`; sidecar cloned by an init container (Phase 3) |\n| Runtime | the role's `Dockerfile` image under Docker if it has one, else a host subprocess; hard timeout | a pod from the role's `Dockerfile` image, or the default `ard-agent` image; hard timeout |\n| Heartbeat | PID alive plus log file mtime | pod phase plus container restarts |\n| Logs | streamed to `~/.ard/logs/<owner>/<repo>/<issue#>/` while running; committed to `.ard/<issue#>/logs/` at close-out | pod logs, committed to `.ard/<issue#>/logs/` at close-out |\n| Labels on resources | job id in the directory name | `ard/issue`, `ard/stage`, `ard/role`, `ard/job` |\n| Good for | Phase 1, one host, fast iteration | parallelism, isolation, untrusted inputs |\n\n**Agent CLIs plug in through adapters.** Each adapter turns (prompt file, context paths, budget) into a command line and parses the exit code and, where the CLI reports them, turns and cost. For Claude Code that is `claude -p --output-format=stream-json --verbose --restricted --safe-mode --strict-mcp-config --disable-slash-commands --permission-mode=dontAsk --settings='{\"includeGitInstructions\":fa\n## Guardrails\n\nAgents with push access read text that strangers can write, so the controls sit in code and permissions, not in prompts.\n\n- **Identity: a GitHub App, not a PAT.** Installation tokens expire hourly and are scoped to contents, issues and pull requests (read and write), plus checks (read and write) and commit statuses (read\n- **The GitHub client never follows redirects.** A redirect can turn a write into a silent GET, replay it into another repository, or carry the token to another host. An issue that redirects to anothe\n- **Agents hold no GitHub token.** The executor does. The agent's environment has only its model credential: an API key or a subscription OAuth token; in Kubernetes a network policy limits egress to t\n- **Intake is configurable (decided, #2).** `intake` is either `all` (every human-filed issue enters, no label) or `label` (only issues with the configured label). Issue templates and forms apply thei\n- **Untrusted text is passed as data.** Issue bodies, comments and PR text go to the agent as files the prompt labels as untrusted input, never spliced into the instructions. `issue.md` omits ard's ow\n- **Planted repo config does not steer agents.** A branch can carry CLAUDE.md, AGENTS.md, `.claude/` settings, hooks, skills, agents or a `.mcp.json`. The Claude adapter's flags stop all of them loadi\n- **Agents write only their own files.** Close-out lets a job change under `.ard/<issue#>/` only its own role file and what its role owns (a `plan.md` section, `design/`); anything else fails the job \n- **Role files are human-owned.** The diff guard and the committer's code lane reject any worker change under `.ard/` or `.github/` (so `.ard/config.yaml` and every role directory), and the merger tre\n- **The tag-writing credential is separate.** Only the `tag` job of `version.yml`, in the `release` environment, holds a token that can push tags, from a release App that must differ from ard's App (t\n- **Merges are gated.** The merger lands a PR only when every review passed and required checks (read from the branch rules API) are green on an up-to-date branch; protected paths wait for a write-acc\n- **Agent text cannot close issues.** Agent text in a commit message, PR title or PR body has closing references neutralised (`committer.NeutraliseRefs`: \"Fixes #12\" becomes \"Fixes issue 12\"). Only a \n- **Worker diffs are scanned.** The worker's diff is checked for known credential values and for secret patterns on the lines the job added. A hit holds the change for a human (the comment names file,\n- **Committed output is redacted.** Close-out strips known secret values, and replaces the controller host's paths (see \"All agent output is committed too\") with placeholders, in each log and every `.\n- **Budgets.** Every job has a dollar cap (`max_budget_usd`) and a hard timeout; every role and the whole repo have a daily dollar cap. The caps are enforced at the pool's claim gate (`internal/budget\n- **Concurrency.** `max_pods` (default 3) caps every job together, and at most one triage job runs per issue at a time, because triage roles share `plan.md`; no triage job is claimed for an issue whil\n- **Kill switch.** Four sources stop new claims, scanner runs (filing and autoclose), stale closes and merges: a local pause file `~/.ard/pause/<owner>/<repo>` outside the repository (the path is lowe\n- **Observability.** Every claim, close-out and failure is an event in local state. A daily digest on the pinned status issue shows queue depth by label, jobs run, failures and spend. Each issue's bod\n## Open questions\n\nAll Phase 0 decisions (#1 to #9) are recorded above.\n\n- [x] **Headless modes for `kimi` and `dsh-tui`.** ([#7](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/issues/7)) Decided: support `kimi`, defer `dsh-tui`. `kimi -p \"<pro\n526:- **Agent image.** The default `ard-agent` image (`deploy/agent/Dockerfile`:","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"311bb2c6-9a43-45e9-b51b-8ab97c5a2cc9","uuid":"687a3c7b-f9f5